  "properties": {
    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "You are a reliability engineer working on a weather app. The app uses a linear regression model to predict the temperature based on the hour of the day. As an initial prototype, you are testing how well your app can model one day of hourly temperatures in degrees Fahrenheit from one weather station.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n1. Train the linear regression model on the inputs (hours) and outputs (temperatures).\r\n2. Compute the model’s prediction for the temperature at 3 pm (hour 15) and at 11 pm (hour 23) and print each prediction. Then, print the actual temperature recorded in the dataset for 3 pm and 11 pm.\r\n3. Run the program and evaluate the result to see what kinds of inputs the model handles well and what kinds of inputs the model doesn’t handle well. You’ll answer a question about this in Level 4.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nUse `model.fit` to train the model on a dataset of inputs and outputs.\r\n\r\nThe `temperatures` list stores all of the temperatures from the training data, sorted by hour. `temperatures[0]` is the temperature recorded at midnight.\r\n\r\nIf `x` is an input value, you can use a fitted model to make a prediction with `model.predict([[x]])[0]`. You can also `round` your answer to avoid long decimals.\r\n",
